1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,579
This video is a general guide to the final project, worth thirty percent of the course and mapped to all four outcomes.

2
00:00:08,579 --> 00:00:12,616
Always follow the brief and rubric from your instructor.

3
00:00:12,616 --> 00:00:19,248
This guide shows how to turn everything you learned into one clear project and presentation.

4
00:00:20,048 --> 00:00:26,731
The final project is a summative project that shows the whole pipeline on one dataset.

5
00:00:26,731 --> 00:00:28,829
It tests all four outcomes:

6
00:00:28,829 --> 00:00:42,116
analysing the dataset, applying univariate, bivariate and multivariate E D A, cleaning and transforming it, and interpreting results into insights that support hypotheses.

7
00:00:42,116 --> 00:00:50,120
Resources include datasets and case studies from domains like insurance, retail, pharma and healthcare.

8
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:53,485
Choose a dataset and a question.

9
00:00:53,485 --> 00:01:01,421
Pick a real dataset from a domain you can explain, such as insurance, retail, pharma or healthcare.

10
00:01:01,421 --> 00:01:08,074
It should have enough rows, a target or outcome you care about, and some real mess:

11
00:01:08,074 --> 00:01:11,040
missing values, outliers, categories.

12
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:16,731
Write the problem statement, the unit of observation, and the sentence:

13
00:01:16,731 --> 00:01:23,464
this sample can support claims about, blank, and cannot support claims about, blank.

14
00:01:24,264 --> 00:01:29,453
Structure the project like an industry notebook.

15
00:01:29,453 --> 00:01:33,128
Title block and executive summary.

16
00:01:33,128 --> 00:01:36,912
Problem, target and success metric.

17
00:01:36,912 --> 00:01:38,966
Refined hypotheses.

18
00:01:38,966 --> 00:01:43,074
Setup with a single seed and versions.

19
00:01:43,074 --> 00:01:44,263
Data audit.

20
00:01:44,263 --> 00:01:46,209
E D A to insights.

21
00:01:46,209 --> 00:01:48,046
Stratified split.

22
00:01:48,046 --> 00:01:51,398
Pipeline with cross-validation.

23
00:01:51,398 --> 00:01:53,452
Test set used once.

24
00:01:53,452 --> 00:01:56,586
Insights and recommendations.

25
00:01:56,586 --> 00:01:59,505
Limitations and next steps.

26
00:01:59,505 --> 00:02:02,424
And a submission checklist.

27
00:02:03,224 --> 00:02:15,031
State clearly, for every headline claim, whether it is exploratory, generated by this analysis, or confirmatory, pre-specified before you looked.

28
00:02:15,031 --> 00:02:20,161
Explore on the training part and confirm only on held-out rows.

29
00:02:20,161 --> 00:02:25,780
If you test many things on one dataset, correct for multiple testing.

30
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:30,584
This honesty is part of what makes the project trustworthy.

31
00:02:31,384 --> 00:02:33,678
Prepare the data carefully.

32
00:02:33,678 --> 00:02:37,246
Audit and fix quality problems with a log.

33
00:02:37,246 --> 00:02:42,259
Explain missing values before imputing and compare methods.

34
00:02:42,259 --> 00:02:44,298
Treat outliers by cause.

35
00:02:44,298 --> 00:02:46,506
Encode categories by type.

36
00:02:46,506 --> 00:02:54,662
Transform skewed columns, and consider feature engineering and dimension reduction if they help.

37
00:02:54,662 --> 00:03:03,328
Put every step that learns from data inside a pipeline fitted on training rows only, to avoid leakage.

38
00:03:04,128 --> 00:03:05,429
Present a story.

39
00:03:05,429 --> 00:03:06,080
Context:

40
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,821
what situation is this and why does it matter.

41
00:03:09,821 --> 00:03:10,553
Conflict:

42
00:03:10,553 --> 00:03:14,864
what did the data reveal that is surprising or wrong.

43
00:03:14,864 --> 00:03:15,759
Resolution:

44
00:03:15,759 --> 00:03:19,256
what do you recommend and what will change.

45
00:03:19,256 --> 00:03:34,872
Use an insight title on each slide, honest axes, one chart per message, and turn each insight into observation, evidence, meaning and action that a manager could use without reading your code.

46
00:03:35,672 --> 00:03:38,576
Check yourself before presenting.

47
00:03:38,576 --> 00:03:41,392
Is the problem and metric clear?

48
00:03:41,392 --> 00:03:43,504
Is every claim labelled?

49
00:03:43,504 --> 00:03:47,376
Is the data audited and the cleaning logged?

50
00:03:47,376 --> 00:03:48,960
Are charts honest?

51
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:53,096
Is the model evaluated on a test set used once?

52
00:03:53,096 --> 00:03:56,704
Can you answer why you chose each method?

53
00:03:56,704 --> 00:03:59,696
Does the project state its limits?

54
00:03:59,696 --> 00:04:04,976
And does the notebook run from top to bottom with no errors?

55
00:04:05,776 --> 00:04:07,225
A few last tips.

56
00:04:07,225 --> 00:04:09,489
Keep the project focused:

57
00:04:09,489 --> 00:04:14,470
one question, done well, beats many half-finished ones.

58
00:04:14,470 --> 00:04:18,002
Practise the presentation with a timer.

59
00:04:18,002 --> 00:04:23,164
Be ready to explain any number, any chart and any choice.

60
00:04:23,164 --> 00:04:25,609
And ask for feedback early.

61
00:04:25,609 --> 00:04:30,952
Good luck with your final project, and thanks for watching.

